  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 8ef4a382a350f98438c21cfd3c789fdf, type: 3}
  m_Name: Snapback
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  lessonTitle: Snapback
  lessonId: snapback
  slides:
  - slideName: Slide 1
    slideType: 0
    bodyText: In this lesson, you will learn about the capture technqiue "snapback".
      You can perform a snapback on your opponent when the group of stones they used
      to capture one of your stones with are in Atari after capture.
    boardSource: 1
    boardJsonFile: {fileID: 0}
    inlineBoardSize: 7
    inlineCurrentPlayer: 2
    inlineBoardFlat: 
    inlineAnnotationTypeFlat: 
    inlineAnnotationNumberFlat: 
    correctYesAnswer: 1
    correctNumberAnswer: 0
  - slideName: Slide 2
    slideType: 0
    bodyText: It's alright if you dont\'t understand. Here's an example! Notice how
      white can capture the black stone in the center by playing at the highlighted
      spot?
    boardSource: 1
    boardJsonFile: {fileID: 0}
    inlineBoardSize: 7
    inlineCurrentPlayer: 2
    inlineBoardFlat: 
    inlineAnnotationTypeFlat: 
    inlineAnnotationNumberFlat: 
    correctYesAnswer: 1
    correctNumberAnswer: 0
  - slideName: Slide 3
    slideType: 0
    bodyText: However, after the capture, see how white is in Atari?
    boardSource: 1
    boardJsonFile: {fileID: 0}
    inlineBoardSize: 7
    inlineCurrentPlayer: 2
    inlineBoardFlat: 
    inlineAnnotationTypeFlat: 
    inlineAnnotationNumberFlat: 
    correctYesAnswer: 1
    correctNumberAnswer: 0
  - slideName: Slide 4
    slideType: 0
    bodyText: This allows black to capture white right back. This is where the name
      "snapback" comes from.
    boardSource: 1
    boardJsonFile: {fileID: 0}
    inlineBoardSize: 7
    inlineCurrentPlayer: 1
    inlineBoardFlat: 
    inlineAnnotationTypeFlat: 
    inlineAnnotationNumberFlat: 
    correctYesAnswer: 1
    correctNumberAnswer: 0
  - slideName: Slide 5
    slideType: 0
    bodyText: To set up a snapback, you'll need to place a stone at an unexpected
      location. Look at this scenario, for example. If black plays at one to put
      the highlighted stones in Atari, white can simply connect at 2 to save them.
    boardSource: 1
    boardJsonFile: {fileID: 0}
    inlineBoardSize: 7
    inlineCurrentPlayer: 1
    inlineBoardFlat: 
    inlineAnnotationTypeFlat: 
    inlineAnnotationNumberFlat: 
    correctYesAnswer: 1
    correctNumberAnswer: 0
  - slideName: Slide 6
    slideType: 0
    bodyText: However, if black plays here, the same snapback setup from earlier
      appears. This stone is called a "throw-in" stone because its whole point is
      to be captured by white to set up a snapback.
    boardSource: 1
    boardJsonFile: {fileID: 0}
    inlineBoardSize: 7
    inlineCurrentPlayer: 1
    inlineBoardFlat: 
    inlineAnnotationTypeFlat: 
    inlineAnnotationNumberFlat: 
    correctYesAnswer: 1
    correctNumberAnswer: 0
  - slideName: Slide 7
    slideType: 1
    bodyText: Here's some practice. Place a throw-in stone to set up a snapback!
    boardSource: 0
    boardJsonFile: {fileID: 4900000, guid: bcf8e79988b258f48b5c79f68acd1434, type: 3}
